3. Technical Principles

Visual-first: users should understand services and choices through visual work rather than long forms.

Progressive complexity: collect data only when the next action requires it.

One project, one main taste: reuse the Main Taste Profile across selected services.

Controlled customization: Ready Design properties define what the user and AI may change.

Human authorship: preserve creator identity, ownership, licences, and compensation metadata.

Explainable matching: show meaningful reasons for recommendations.

Small recommendation sets: return no more than six options.

Modular MVP: isolate domains without introducing microservice complexity.

Provider abstraction: AI, storage, export, and communication providers must be replaceable behind interfaces.

Auditable decisions: record important permission, AI, export, matching, and project-state events.

4. System Architecture

4.1 Recommended MVP Architecture

Use a modular monolith for the MVP:

React single-page application for the web interface.

Flask REST API for application and domain logic.

PostgreSQL for relational and structured JSON data.

Object storage for images, portfolio assets, uploads, previews, and deliverables.

Background worker for exports, image processing, and longer AI operations.

AI adapter layer for optional external or locally hosted model providers.

This structure is simpler to develop, test, and deploy than microservices while keeping domain boundaries clear enough for future extraction.

