12. Matching and Team Recommendation

12.1 Candidate Filtering

Before scoring, remove candidates that do not meet mandatory conditions:

Creative profile is not approved.

No selected service is offered.

Required portfolio evidence is missing.

Creative is suspended or hidden.

Do not require price, location, or availability at the initial discovery stage unless the user explicitly supplies these filters later.

12.2 Initial Single-Creative Score

The MVP may use the following configurable starting weights:

match_score =

0.50 × taste_similarity

0.30 × service_coverage

0.15 × sector_relevance

0.05 × portfolio_evidence

Factor

Meaning

Taste similarity

Weighted similarity between effective project taste and Creative DNA.

Service coverage

Proportion and priority of selected services covered.

Sector relevance

Evidence of relevant sector work.

Portfolio evidence

Strength and quality of tagged approved work supporting the match.

Weights are configuration data attached to an algorithm_version.

12.3 Taste Similarity

Use cosine similarity for normalized tag vectors in the MVP:

similarity(A, B) = (A · B) / (||A|| × ||B||)

For a service with a custom direction, use the effective service taste rather than the unchanged Main Taste Profile.

12.4 Team Recommendation

Team composition is considered only when no strong single creative or studio covers the selected services.

The MVP team builder should:

Generate a small candidate pool per uncovered service.

Combine candidates until required service coverage is reached.

Reject combinations above the configured maximum team size.

Calculate project-taste fit for every member.

Calculate visual coherence between members.

Penalize unnecessary team size.

team_score =

0.40 × project_taste_fit

0.30 × service_coverage

0.20 × member_coherence

0.10 × sector_relevance

team_size_penalty

For the MVP, a team should normally contain no more than three creatives unless the scope is formally expanded.

12.5 Result Diversification

The final six options should not be near-duplicates. A diversification step may limit repeated creatives, studios, or extremely similar style vectors.

12.6 Recommendation Explanation

Every option should include concise reasons, for example:

Matches the project's bold, warm, and typographic direction.

Covers identity, menu, and packaging.

Includes relevant Food and Beverage work.

Proposed team maintains a shared visual direction across selected services.

Do not expose raw internal weights as if they were objective guarantees.

