27. Stage 3 Conclusion

The TENTORA MVP should be implemented as a modular React and Flask application backed by PostgreSQL, object storage, and asynchronous processing for exports and selected AI actions.

The architecture supports two distinct but connected journeys:

Design Now: sector → service → ready design → controlled customization → preview → export.

Work With a Creative: sector → services → one project taste → up to six compatible creative or team options → selection → workspace → delivery.

The system's most important technical rules are:

Ready Designs are structured, versioned, and permission-controlled.

AI may modify only approved properties and must return validated results.

One Main Taste Profile is reused across a multi-service project.

A service-specific direction is stored as an override, not a repeated full test.

Creative DNA is derived from approved portfolio evidence and versioned.

Recommendation logic is explainable, configurable, and limited to six options.

A team is one recommendation option and is used only when needed for service coverage.

Creator rights, licences, and AI permissions are first-class data.

Anonymous work can become authenticated ownership without losing progress.

A saved self-service design can move into human creative execution without restarting.

This document provides the technical baseline for Stage 4 MVP development. The open decisions in Section 26 must be approved before their related features are implemented.
